Get your first widget running in 30 seconds.<br/><br/><b>HTML:</b><pre class='code-block'>&lt;h1 id="hello"&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;</pre><b>CSS:</b><pre class='code-block'>h1 {
    color: #3b82f6;
}</pre><b>JS:</b><pre class='code-block'>document.getElementById("hello").textContent = "Hello Widget!";</pre>

The widget runtime automatically applies a clean CSS reset:<pre class='code-block'>html,
body {
    margin: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    background: transparent;
}</pre><b>Background Transparency:</b> By default, the sandbox document background is transparent. This allows your widget to adopt a glassmorphic aesthetic matching the startpage wallpaper behind it. To preserve transparency, avoid setting solid background colors on your parent elements; use translucent backgrounds like <code>background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05)</code> and <code>backdrop-filter: blur(12px)</code>.

To build widgets that look perfect in both Light and Dark modes, the sandbox body is automatically injected with the class names <code>widget light</code> or <code>widget dark</code> matching the parent startpage theme.<br/><br/><b>Secured CSS Theme Selection:</b> Scope your CSS classes using the parent namespace prefix to apply mode-specific styling:<br/><br/><pre class='code-block'>/* Dark Theme (Default) */
.widget.dark .weather-card {
    background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6);
    color: #f8fafc;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
}

/* Light Theme */
.widget.light .weather-card {
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6);
    color: #0f172a;
    border: 1px solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
}</pre>

Control browser audio and video playback safely from your custom widgets across YouTube, Spotify, SoundCloud, Netflix, and HTML5 media players without requiring raw extension privileges:<br/><br/><ul><li><code>widget.media.getAudibleTabs()</code>: Returns a Promise resolving to an array of tabs currently playing sound with <code>{ id, title, url, favIconUrl, audible, muted }</code>.</li><li><code>widget.media.getState()</code>: Returns a Promise resolving to the current aggregate media state object: <code>{ isPlaying, audibleTabsCount, currentTab, tabs }</code>.</li><li><code>widget.media.togglePlayPause(tabId)</code>: Toggles playback on the specified tab (or the active playing tab if omitted).</li><li><code>widget.media.nextTrack(tabId)</code>: Skips forward / plays next track (+10s for video/audio).</li><li><code>widget.media.prevTrack(tabId)</code>: Skips back / plays previous track (-10s for video/audio).</li><li><code>widget.media.toggleMute(tabId)</code>: Toggles audio mute on the specified tab.</li><li><code>widget.media.focusTab(tabId)</code>: Activates and focuses the tab and browser window.</li><li><code>widget.media.onMediaChanged(callback)</code>: Subscribes to real-time media state changes broadcast by the browser.</li></ul><br/><b>Example:</b><pre class='code-block'>// Check playing state and toggle playback
const state = await widget.media.getState();
if (state.isPlaying) {
    await widget.media.togglePlayPause();
}

// Subscribe to live audio state changes
widget.media.onMediaChanged((state) => {
    console.log('Active media changed:', state);
    document.getElementById('track-name').textContent = state.currentTab?.title || 'Idle';
});</pre>

Avoid hardcoding credentials inside your JavaScript files. Write your secrets line-by-line using standard key-value assignments in the Environment Variables input field:<pre class='code-block'>API_KEY=wx_a4b9c1d680
DEFAULT_CITY=Tokyo</pre>These parameters are parsed and injected securely. Access them directly in your JavaScript using:<pre class='code-block'>const key = ENV.API_KEY;
const city = ENV.DEFAULT_CITY;</pre>
